From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 23:52:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] BD-J host: the host side and hades-bdj-host

bd_bdj_host_run(): what the host process runs on its one connection. It opens the disc with
bd_open_files(), whose files are requests to the player, and plays the player's calls one at a
time in the order they come, as the player's BLURAY would see them; a receiver thread hands the
answers of the player to the threads that wait for them (libbluray reads the disc from the
calling thread and from the Java threads). Small reads of a file are served from a read-ahead of
128 KiB, so that a stream takes one request to the player per 128 KiB and not one per aligned
unit. HDMV overlays are sent inline, marked with the call they were drawn in; an ARGB update is
drawn into the shared memory and waits for the player's acknowledgement before the next one.
Only the host decides its Java, cache and persistent storage; an ARGB plane larger than the
shared memory is not shown and said in the log. Every log line of libbluray goes to the player.
When the player closes the disc, the disc is closed while the player is still connected (the
CLOSE of the BD-J planes reaches it), then the connection and the process end.

hades-bdj-host is the program (src/tools, -Dhades_bdj_host=enabled, installed in
<libdir>/hades-bdj). It takes the connection from systemd (LISTEN_FDS=1, descriptor 3, or
standard input with StandardInput=socket) or --fd, and isolates itself with
hades_bdj_sandbox_enter() before it reads anything; it is linked with the sandbox and its check
(bdj_sandbox.c, standalone) and libseccomp. It gives the disc /usr/lib/hades-bdj/jre,
/var/lib/hades-bdj/persistent if it is writable, and /tmp/hades-bdj-cache. Builds for tests
(-Dhades_bdj_test=true, never in an image) also take --no-sandbox.
